## Tracearr MCP Tools

Always use the `tracearr` MCP tools for Tracearr data — never shell scripts.

- `get_health` — server connectivity, version, online/offline per server. Use for "is Tracearr up?"
- `get_stats` — dashboard overview: active streams, total users, 30-day sessions, 7-day violations. Use for "how many users/sessions?"
- `get_stats_today` — today's plays, watch time, alerts, active users (timezone-aware). Also "how many today?"
- `get_activity` — trends: plays over time, concurrent streams, day/hour distributions, platforms, quality. Use for "what are people watching most?"
- `get_streams` — active playback sessions with full codec/quality/device details. Use for "is anyone watching right now?"
- `get_users` — paginated user list with trust scores, session counts, last activity.
- `get_violations` — sharing detection violations with rule info and severity. Use for "any account sharing?"
- `get_history` — session history with codec details, filterable by date/type/state. Use for "show me watch history."

## Sonarr MCP Tools

Always use the `sonarr` MCP tools for Sonarr.

- `get_queue` — active downloads: series, episode, quality, progress, status, errors. Use for "what's downloading?"
- `get_series` — list all shows or search by title (id, title, year, status, seasons, size). Use for "what shows do I have?" / "find show X". With a `title` it hits the *lookup* endpoint and returns TVDB metadata even for shows NOT in the library (those entries have no Sonarr `id`) — never say "it's not in the library so I can't add it".
- `get_episodes` — episodes for a series (id, title, season/episode, air date, monitored, `episodeFileId`, plus the embedded `episodeFile` object with its exact `relative_path` when `include_episode_file=true`, the default).
- `add_series` — add by **TVDB id** (`tvdb_id` from `get_series` lookup). Defaults to first quality profile / root folder if omitted. Pass `search_for_missing_episodes=false` to add without searching (approval-gated otherwise, see Download Authorization Policy). Use `list_quality_profiles` (id → `quality_profile_id`) and `list_root_folders` (path e.g. `/tv` → `root_folder_path`, shows `freeSpace`) when you need to be specific.
- `search_releases` — interactive indexer search for a specific episode. Use for "search for episode X" / "find better quality" (get series/episode ids first).
- `grab_release` — push a specific searched release to the download client using its `guid` and `indexerId`. Approval-gated.
- `trigger_episode_search` — Sonarr picks the best release for given episodes. Use for "search for missing episodes." Approval-gated.
- `trigger_series_search` — re-search all monitored episodes in a series. Approval-gated.
- `get_episode_file` — read ONE episode file's details (exact `relative_path`, size, quality) without deleting. Use right before `delete_episode_file` to build the path confirmation.
- `delete_episode_file` — delete ONE episode file from disk, keeping the series and episode record. Get `episodeFileId` from `get_episodes` first, then read the exact path via `get_episodes` (embedded `episodeFile.relative_path`) or `get_episode_file` before confirming. Destructive: confirm with the user, echo the exact path in the confirmation.
- `delete_series` — remove the whole library entry and its files. The nuclear option; confirm with the user.
- `blocklist_queue_item` — remove a queue item and blocklist the release so it won't be grabbed again. Also removes it from the download client. Confirm before calling; find the queue item id with `get_queue`.

## Radarr MCP Tools

Always use the `radarr` MCP tools for Radarr.

- `get_queue` — active downloads: movie title, quality, progress, status, errors.
- `get_movie` — list all movies or search by title (id, title, year, status, size, `movieFile.id`). With a `title` it hits the *lookup* endpoint and returns TMDB metadata even for movies NOT in the library (those entries have no Radarr `id`) — use `add_movie` for those.
- `add_movie` — add by **TMDB id** (`tmdbId` from `get_movie` lookup). Defaults to first quality profile / root folder if omitted. Pass `search_for_movie=false` to add without searching (approval-gated otherwise). `list_quality_profiles` → `quality_profile_id`, `list_root_folders` (e.g. `/movies`) → `root_folder_path`.
- `search_releases` — interactive indexer search for a specific movie. Use for "search for movie X" / "find better quality".
- `grab_release` — push a searched release to the download client via `guid` + `indexerId`. Approval-gated.
- `trigger_movie_search` — Radarr picks the best release for given movies. Use for "re-search this movie." Approval-gated.
- `delete_movie_file` — delete ONE movie file, keeping the movie entry. Get `movieFile.id` from `get_movie` first. Destructive: confirm, echo the path.
- `delete_movie` — remove the whole library entry and its files. Nuclear option; confirm with the user.

## Loki Log Queries

Use the Grafana MCP tools to query Loki. The Loki datasource UID is `{{ hermes_plexibot_loki_uid }}`.

**Label reality check.** Available labels: `container_name`, `filename`, `host`, `hostname`, `job`, `level`, `source`, `syslog_identifier`, `transport`, `unit`. `source` values are `docker`, `file`, `journal`. `container_name` IS available for most containers (`plex`, `sonarr`, `radarr`, `prowlarr`, `sabnzbd`, `tracearr`, `jellyfin`, `jellyseerr`, ...). **Plex logs are scraped from the log file**, so they appear under `container_name="plex"` with `source="file"` — NOT `source="docker"`; querying `{source="docker", host="{{ plex_host }}"}` returns nothing. Arr stack containers on `{{ media_stack_host }}` generally appear under `container_name="<service>"` with `source="docker"`.

{% raw -%}
Useful LogQL patterns:

```logql
{container_name="plex"}
{container_name="plex"} |~ "(?i)(transcode|transcod)"
{container_name="sonarr"}
{container_name="radarr"}
{container_name="prowlarr"}
{container_name="sabnzbd"}
{container_name=~"plex|sonarr|radarr|prowlarr|sabnzbd|tracearr"} |~ "(?i)(error|fatal|panic)" !~ "(?i)(no error|without error|error_count=0)"
{container_name=~"sonarr|radarr|sabnzbd|qbittorrent"} |~ "(?i)(download|import|grab)"
```

Tips: prefer `container_name` over `host` (more precise); if a query returns nothing, run `list_loki_label_values` for `container_name` to confirm the exact name; always check `query_loki_stats` before pulling logs to gauge volume.

{% endraw %}## Prometheus Queries (QNAP Storage)

Use the Grafana MCP tools to query Prometheus. The Prometheus datasource UID is `{{ hermes_plexibot_prometheus_uid }}`.

The QNAP NAS is monitored via SNMP exporter. The Multimedia volume (where Plex media lives) is `volumeIndex="{{ hermes_plexibot_qnap_multimedia_volume_index }}"`. Raw values are in **kilobytes**.

```promql
volumeFreeSize{volumeIndex="{{ hermes_plexibot_qnap_multimedia_volume_index }}", job="{{ hermes_plexibot_qnap_long_job }}"} / 1024 / 1024 / 1024
volumeCapacity{volumeIndex="{{ hermes_plexibot_qnap_multimedia_volume_index }}", job="{{ hermes_plexibot_qnap_long_job }}"} / 1024 / 1024 / 1024
100 - (volumeFreeSize{volumeIndex="{{ hermes_plexibot_qnap_multimedia_volume_index }}", job="{{ hermes_plexibot_qnap_long_job }}"} / volumeCapacity{volumeIndex="{{ hermes_plexibot_qnap_multimedia_volume_index }}", job="{{ hermes_plexibot_qnap_long_job }}"} * 100)
```

Volume index reference: {{ hermes_plexibot_qnap_volume_reference }}

**Always use `job="{{ hermes_plexibot_qnap_long_job }}"`** — the short scrape job (`{{ hermes_plexibot_qnap_short_job }}`) is unreliable due to timeout flapping.

